{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>For his sin with which he sinned.</b> The reason that in the offering of a rich person it says (v. 6) ״for his sin,״ whereas in the offering of a poor person it says ״for his sin with which he sinned,״ will be explained based on what is explained in Torat Kohanim (Leviticus 5:13) regarding the offering of extreme poverty (dalei dalut): One might think that the most severe among them should be brought with a sheep or a goat, the lighter ones with turtledoves and young pigeons, and the lightest of the light with a tenth of an ephah — Scripture therefore says ״from one of these״ to equate the lighter ones with the more severe ones, etc., end quote. That is, since there are in this passage three types of sins: defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, which carries the punishment of excision (karet) — this is the most severe of all; and the oath of testimony, for which one is liable for its intentional and its unwitting commission — this is lighter than the preceding and more severe than the one following it, namely the oath of utterance, for which one is liable only for its unwitting commission — one might think that the more severe ones, etc." "It is difficult: how could a person say thus, when the verse says ״and if his means do not suffice״ etc. — which implies that the verse is speaking of the sin itself for which one is liable to bring a sheep or a goat. And likewise ״if his means do not suffice״ etc. regarding the extreme poverty. I saw that the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrahi, explained ״and if his means do not suffice״ — that his hand did not reach the offense for which one is liable to bring a sheep, etc., but rather a lighter offense than that mentioned in the passage, he shall bring two turtledoves, etc.; and if his means did not suffice for the offense for which one is obligated to bring two young pigeones, etc. — end. According to this approach, the verse's saying <b>for his sin with which he sinned</b> is resolved, on the grounds that this division is the division of the lighter sins, which is the oath of testimony — so that you should not err by thinking that this bird-offering is only for this lighter sin, but for one more severe than it, namely defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, no bird-offering is brought even in poverty — Scripture therefore says ״for his sin with which he sinned,״ meaning two sins are stated: one for the oath of testimony, and one for defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, so that you shall not err regarding it as mentioned. And the verse returned to the passage of extreme poverty and extended it even to the matter of the tenth of an ephah, which applies to all three. However, the interpretation of the RaM, of blessed memory, appears to me extremely forced, for how could reason accept interpreting ״if his means do not suffice״ as referring to a lighter sin — the notion is considered far-fetched." "<b>Alternatively,</b> we may explain the Tanna's intent along these lines: had the verse not said ״from one of these,״ I would have said that the most severe among them, which is defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, has its offering fixed as a sheep, and what the verse said — ״and if his means do not suffice for a sheep, and he brings turtledoves or young pigeons״ — he is not saying regarding all of them, but only regarding the lightest of all of them, the one whose atonement He lightened — that although in his wealth He obligated him to bring a sheep, in his poverty, if his means do not suffice for a sheep, he shall bring turtledoves. And this would serve to show that it also applies to the more severe sin — proof must be brought. And likewise His saying ״and if his means do not suffice for two turtledoves״ etc., he shall bring a tenth of an ephah — I would have said that He is speaking regarding the lightest of the light, that this offering applies, which is the oath of utterance, but not regarding the oath of testimony, which is more severe — and needless to say, not for defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, that HaShem would not lighten it for him to atone with a tenth of an ephah. And I would seek to strengthen this from what we find, that HaShem fixed a sheep or goat offering for all unwitting sins, as stated in the preceding passage — why would this excision of defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things be diminished? Surely the ascending and descending offering comes only for the lighter ones. And it is along these lines: the rich person brings for all of them a sheep or goat; the poor person — the verse lightened it for him to bring turtledoves and young pigeones for the oath of testimony, but for defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things he does not leave the category of the sins for which He obligated him in the preceding passage, as it is written ״a soul, etc., when one sins unwittingly״; extreme poverty — the verse lightened it for him to bring a tenth of an ephah for the oath of utterance, but for the oath of testimony, which is more severe, he does not bring a tenth of an ephah. And this is the core of the error in which I would err in the matter, for Scripture says ״from one of these״ — behold, the verse equated all of them, to bring even for defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things a tenth of an ephah, and all the more so turtledoves and young pigeones. And this is the intent of the Tanna when he said ״one might think.״ But what he said regarding equating the lighter ones to the more severe ones is by way of context, and the core of the error for which the verse comes is not that, but rather equating the more severe ones to the lighter ones." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to me to explain the Tanna's words along these lines: since the verse arranged three types of sins, and it appears to me that there are in them three gradations, and He came to arrange the sins that require atonement. He began with the most severe of all and said ״he shall bring a sheep,״ and if his means do not suffice, he shall bring the offering that relates to what precedes it, which is the oath of testimony — ״two turtledoves, etc., or two young pigeones,״ which applies to the oath of testimony both in wealth and in poverty, and to defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things it applies in poverty but not in wealth. And His saying ״and if his means do not suffice״ — meaning, regarding the oath of testimony He is saying that turtledoves and young pigeones are fixed for it; if his means do not suffice, then this one brings the offering fixed for the oath of utterance." "You therefore find: for the oath of utterance he brings a tenth of an ephah as a fixed offering, even if he is wealthy; for the oath of testimony, in his wealth he brings turtledoves and young pigeones, and in his poverty he brings a tenth of an ephah; for defilement of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things, a rich person brings a sheep or goat, and in his poverty he brings turtledoves and young pigeones but not a tenth of an ephah. And this is the statement of the Tanna: ״the more severe among them shall be with a sheep״ — meaning, they are the ones who shall bring a sheep, and not the lighter ones, which are the oath of testimony and the oath of utterance, even in their wealth. And the Tanna was not concerned that you might err by thinking they should be with a sheep only and not with turtledoves and young pigeones, for you have beside you ״and if his means do not suffice״ — but rather to say that the lighter ones are not with a sheep. And His saying ״the lighter ones with a bird״ — meaning, they shall bring them, and not the lightest of the light, which are those of the oath of utterance, who shall bring a bird but rather a tenth of an ephah. And He was not concerned that you might err by thinking that for the oath of testimony one does not bring a tenth of an ephah in poverty, for it is written ״and if his means do not suffice״ etc." "You are thus made to say that a lamb offering applies only for the grave [offense] and not for two lighter [ones], and a bird [offering] — for the lightest of the light ones, only for the light one. And when it says that the lightest of the light ones [are brought] with a tenth of an ephah, meaning, and not the grave one, which is the impurity of the Sanctuary and its holy things, with a tenth of an ephah. And do not be concerned that one might err and think it comes to exclude the oath of testimony, for it is explicitly written ״וְאִם לֹא תַשִּׂיג״ adjacent to it. Also, one cannot say that it comes to exclude that there is no lightest-of-the-light in turtledoves and young pigeons, and all the more so in a lamb, for this matter is precisely what it explained by stating that the grave ones among them involve a lamb, to exclude the light ones — and all the more so the lightest of the light; and similarly for birds, this is what it restricted by stating that the light ones among birds [come] to exclude the lightest of the light from birds. You are thus made to say: the oath of utterance applies only with a tenth of an ephah and not with turtledoves, and all the more so not with a lamb; and the oath of testimony applies only with turtledoves and young pigeons, and for a poor person with a tenth of an ephah, and not with a lamb; and the impurity of the Sanctuary and its holy things does not apply at all with a tenth of an ephah but rather with the wealth [to bring] a lamb, and for a poor person turtledoves and young pigeones. It was necessary to say ״מֵאַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה״ — meaning, from the three offenses stated — he shall bring a tenth of an ephah for each one of the three. And they also equated the light ones to the grave ones through the statement ״מֵאַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה״ in this manner: ״עַל חַטָּאתוֹ אֲשֶׁר חָטָא מֵאַחַת״ — meaning, מֵאַחַת, that it should be not only upon the light ones alone but upon all of them. ״מֵאֵלֶּה״ went back and equated them a second time, to say that these are equal in [the matter of] bringing a lamb offering even for the lightest of the light ones when he has wealth. Or the Tanna reasons that the statement ״אַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה״ equated them with respect to everything, so that what is in this one is in that one. And one should not say that the Tanna reasons: since the verse equated the grave one to the light one regarding a tenth of an ephah, all the more so the light one with a lamb — this is not so, for we are dealing with the question of obligation, to obligate him, and what source do we have to obligate him?" "<b>Further,</b> it appears one may say that the Tanna derives the equation of the light ones to the grave ones — that they [all] bring a lamb — from what the verse stated at the beginning of the section (Leviticus 5:5): ״וְהָיָה כִי יֶאְשַׁם לְאַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה וגו׳ וְהֵבִיא אֶת אֲשָׁמוֹ וגו׳ כִּשְׂבָּה״ — behold, it equated those light ones to the grave ones; and here it equated the grave ones to the light ones. And although it established the derivation from one verse, it relied on another verse to raise the question, and it established both distinctions in one place — and such is the way of the Tanna." "<b>And with this</b> we have no need for the forced explanation of R. Eliyahu Mizrachi (Ra'em), who said that the first verse comes to equate them only with respect to the confession mentioned there — for this is forced, since when the verse equated them and said ״וְהִתְוַדָּה וְהֵבִיא אֶת אֲשָׁמוֹ״, where do you find [grounds] to distinguish and say that it equated them only for the matter of leaning? For by what did the verse distinguish between the statement [of confession] and ״וְהֵבִיא אֶת אֲשָׁמוֹ״? On the contrary — the vav connects them!" "<b>And from now on</b> we may come to draw an inference from its saying ״מֵחַטָּאתוֹ אֲשֶׁר חָטָא״ in this manner: lest you say that this bird-offering applies only for the light one, which is the oath of testimony, but not for the grave one, which is the impurity of the Sanctuary and its holy things — it was necessary to say ״אֲשֶׁר חָטָא״ to include even a grave offense. And when it mentioned the section of the extremely destitute, it also included a tenth of an ephah for all of them, through the statement ״מֵאַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה״ — even for the impurity of the Sanctuary and its holy things." "<b>And if</b> you say: let Him inform us regarding the offering of the extremely destitute that there is [such an offering] for the grave [offense], and all the more so [there should be] a poor [person's] offering. It appears one may say that on the grounds that for the extremely destitute it said ״וְאִם לֹא תַשִּׂיג יָדוֹ לִשְׁתֵּי תוֹרִים״ and did not say as it said in the section of poverty ״וְאִם לֹא תַגִּיעַ יָדוֹ דֵּי שֶׂה״, and they derived in Torat Kohanim: even if he has a lamb, if he lacks his [basic] needs, behold this one brings a poor [person's] offering — and we explained this above." "<b>Now behold,</b> the Tanna's derivation was from the fact that Scripture did not say, as it says here, ״וְאִם לֹא תַשִּׂיג יָדוֹ לִשְׁתֵּי תוֹרִים״ — and so the Baal Korban Aharon explained, and it is straightforward. If so, here too, where Scripture did not say ״דֵּי שְׁתֵּי תוֹרִים,״ the matter proves that if he does have them, he is obligated to bring them, even if he needs them [for his own livelihood]. And from this we now understand why Scripture needed to include birds that he may bring for the severe case — namely, contamination of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things — even though he had a lamb, if he needs it [the lamb]. Understand this." "<b>Also,</b> what Rabbenu Hillel explained — ״וְאֵין לוֹ צְרָכָיו״ — as referring to a lamb, our words are justified, for he thereby informed us that this exemption applies even to the severe cases, which are contamination of the Sanctuary and its consecrated things — something that would not have been derivable from the meal offering of a tenth of an ephah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {5 "The passage is a dense halakhic analysis of the gradations of sin-offerings; I have rendered קלים/קלי קלים/חמור as light/lightest-of-the-light/grave throughout, and דלות/דלי דלות as poverty/extreme poverty, to maintain the hierarchical rabbinic terminology.", 7 "רא״ם resolved as R. Eliyahu Mizrachi (Ra'em), a standard abbreviation for this commentator. I read סמוך ממש here in the context of Ra'em's argument as referring to the act of leaning (סמיכה) on the offering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:11.133162326Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}